Hello, David. Let’s look at the rules for compressing text.

The material says:
- A group of the same character with more than 1 repeat is written as `<number><character>`.
- A group of exactly 1 repeat is written only as `<character>`, no number.

Here is the Swift code that follows these rules:

```swift
func encode_runs(_ text: String) -> String {
    var result = ""
    var count = 1
    let characters = Array(text)

    for i in 1..<characters.count {
        if characters[i] == characters[i-1] {
            count += 1
        } else {
            if count > 1 {
                result += "\(count)\(characters[i-1])"
            } else {
                result += "\(characters[i-1])"
            }
            count = 1
        }
    }

    // Handle the last group
    if count > 1 {
        result += "\(count)\(characters.last!)"
    } else {
        result += "\(characters.last!)"
    }

    return result
}
```

This code uses the rules:
- It counts repeats.
- It writes `<number><character>` for repeats > 1.
- It writes `<character>` for repeats = 1.

